          "g": 0.0,
          "i": 1.0
        },
        "species": 39,
        "text": "In an eyetracker study of jumping spiders, the retinas inside the two big front eyes moved, and the spiders spent more time looking at still or slowly moving images. If you are a bold jumping spider, your front eyes need guidance from the pair of eyes beside them to find and follow a moving object, but they scan a still shape without that guidance. Nobody has measured the eye movements of most other jumping spiders, so how your own retinas move is unknown.",
        "cites": [
          {
            "id": "jumper#112",
            "taxon": "Phidippus audax",
            "trait": "eye_movement",
            "grade": "m",
            "source": "Jakob 2018"
          },
          {
            "id": "jumper#219",
            "taxon": "Salticidae",
            "trait": "eye_movement",
            "grade": "m",
            "source": "Bruce 2019"
          }
        ],
        "note": "Left out the fovea: R5's quote does not state the receptor density figure, and R3 covers only Lyssomanes. R2 (Marpissa marina priming) left out for length.",
        "attempt": 2
      },
      {
        "title": "Color",
